    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe new `EnqueueWelcomeNotification` is placed in the right unit and reads as a natural sibling of the existing pattern:\n\n- **Correct granularity.** It's a single small function class with one public `service` method \u2014 matching the codebase convention (`SaveOwner`, `AuditOwnerCreated` both use `service(@Val Owner ...)`). Method name is idiomatic here, not a Servlet-flavored slip.\n- **No problematic duplication.** It mirrors `AuditOwnerCreated`'s shape (dedicated named logger constant, `@Val Owner`, doc explaining placement), but the concern is genuinely distinct \u2014 a separate `NOTIFY` logger with a different purpose than `AUDIT`. Folding notification into the audit class would conflate two independent side-effect channels and violate the one-step-per-class idiom. Keeping them separate is the additive, right call.\n- **Wiring is clean.** Inserted as its own `notify` step between `audit` and `respond`, on the create branch only (not the idempotent-repeat `respondExisting` path), and downstream of `save`/`memberId`/`dedupeMemberId` so id and memberId are settled \u2014 exactly what the doc comment claims. Consistent with `audit` in omitting `govern: [ transaction ]` for a post-persistence side effect.\n- The doc comment matches the surrounding thorough style and correctly cross-references the steps it depends on.\n\nNothing here is becoming a catch-all, and a maintainer of this pipeline would find the addition natural.",
